0.  WHAT CHANGED, AND WHAT DID NOT
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Two rows added -- `fill b top' and `fill c top' -- plus a CONTRACT block and constants.
All fourteen existing row() calls are BYTE-IDENTICAL: verified mechanically by extracting
every `^row(' call from both files with a paren-matching walk and testing set membership.
    ORIGINAL row() calls: 14   REPAIRED: 16   (delta +2)
    ORIGINAL calls NOT byte-identically present in REPAIRED: 0
The whole diff is 4 removed lines (the stale "IS NOW A 14-ROW DETECTOR ... scores 14/14"
banner, which the row addition falsifies) and 120 added.  Both files are LF-only, 0 CRLF.


1.  SIBLING PARITY
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The siblings here are the CASE LABELS of `switch (dst_tlb)', which is the reading the
pcc2widthkills adjudication settled: "at a block that serves TWO case labels the siblings
are the LABELS".  This block serves THREE -- MTE_TLB_A, MTE_TLB_B, MTE_TLB_C -- and they
share one body, so parity is exact rather than analogical.

    label        row before this repair      row after
    MTE_TLB_A    `fill top'                  `fill top'          (unchanged)
    MTE_TLB_B    *** NONE ***                `fill b top'
    MTE_TLB_C    *** NONE ***                `fill c top'

ASYMMETRY FOUND AND CLOSED: it was 1 of 3.  A census over the ROW NAMES -- the thing the
pcc2widthkills corollary says would have shown the gap before the table ran -- shows no
row name in the 14-row set contains a label token at all, which is the tell.

The OTHER label-shaped predicate in the same function, `if (src > MTE_TLB_C)', induces a
TWO-valued partition of which exactly ONE value reaches the edited line, so it owes ONE
cell and that cell is already rowed (`copy top' etc. all drive src_tlb == MTE_TLB_A).
It is NOT three cells.  Recording that here because it is what stops the label rule from
demanding a source-label row set as well.

The LINEAR labels (MTE_TLB_LIN_A/B) are a DIFFERENT block, not siblings of this one, and
they terminate the process at depth 32 -- so a row there would be host death, not a red
row.  N/A with its reason, not a blank.


2.  CLAIM -> ROW  (semantic trigger: does this sentence assert something a row could
    observe?  The five-verb grep is an EXAMPLE, not the test.)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The #509 comment's load-bearing sentence is a UNIVERSAL over this block's three labels:

    "So both assignments were dead, EVERY TILED TRANSFER took the CI arm, and at 32 bits
     CI assigns buf[0] alone while memory_rw copies four -- publishing three bytes of host
     stack into guest memory."

Before this repair the row set instantiated that universal at ONE label of three.  After
it, at three of three, at the depth the repair changes.  That is the claim this repair
discharges, and it is the reason the repair is two rows and not one.

Sentences added by THIS change and where each lands:

  "the `dst_tlb' axis is CLOSED at depth 32 across all three labels"
        -> rows `fill top', `fill b top', `fill c top'.
  "THE TILE VALUE IS THE SAME 0x8002 IN ALL THREE, so DEST does not move"
        -> observable: all three rows read the SAME address; if it moved, the paint
           (0x99) would survive at the read address and the row would red naming it.
  "NEITHER ROW IS GATED ... dest[0] is buf[0], which BOTH colour arms assign"
        -> observable under M1/M2/M4, where the new rows must report 0x44 (a DEFINED
           CI byte) and not "n/a".  A gate would have made them report n/a instead;
           the table below distinguishes those two outcomes.
  "A source-side label condition escapes: every copy row drives src_tlb == MTE_TLB_A"
        -> deliberately NO ROW.  Explicit no-row sentence, in the CONTRACT, under
           clause (e).  Stated so the next reader does not have to rediscover it.


3.  GUARD FIELD vs BODY FIELD
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
GUARD (the repaired comparison):     `depth'
DOMINATING GUARD (the switch):       `dst_tlb'
BODY (the two guarded assignments):  `src_mode', `dst_mode'  (lvalues written)
BODY, values read to build them:     `src_tlb', `dst_tlb', `mode & MTE_MODE_DEPTH_MASK'

WHERE THEY DIFFER: `dst_tlb' is read by the BODY -- `dst_mode = (dst_tlb << 10) + ...',
four lines above the guard -- and is ALSO the operand of the dominating switch.  That is
precisely the joint the DEPENDS-ON closure names, and it is why the 7g-1 ruling is FLOOR
rather than clause-(e) debt.

WRITERS, because reachability of a joint cell is derived from the WRITERS of each lvalue
and never from a steady-state invariant:
    `dst_tlb'  <- (mode & MTE_MODE_DST_BUF_MASK 0x1c) >> 2, i.e. a guest MTE_MODE write.
    `re_tlb_b' <- dev_sgi_re.c's `relative_addr >= CRIME_RE_TLB_B' arm, the exact mirror
                  of the TLB_A arm the shipped probe already drives.
    `re_tlb_c' <- likewise at CRIME_RE_TLB_C.
So the disagreeing state (dst_tlb != 0 while the repair is conditioned on dst_tlb == 0) is
guest-constructible with two constants changed, and was MEASURED so at rung 3 on
2026-09-09: killtable3_20260909/reach_M_tlbA.raw reads A=RGBA, B=CI, C=CI.


4.  CHANNEL -- per NEW row, the mutant it kills and the observable that differs under it
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  fill b top   kills M_tlbA (`&& dst_tlb == MTE_TLB_A') and M_tlbAB is NOT killed by it
               (M_tlbAB admits B).  Channel: dest[0] of a tiled 32-bit fill whose mode
               word carries MTE_MODE_DST_BUF = 1.  0x11 (RGBA, repaired) vs 0x44 (CI,
               defect live).  CAN IT VARY UNDER ITS NAMED MUTANT?  Yes, measured already:
               reach_M_tlbA.raw shows B = CI under exactly this mutant, against a base of
               RGBA.  This row is NOT a survival oracle and NOT a distinctness assertion.
  fill c top   kills M_tlbA AND M_tlbAB.  Same channel at DST_BUF = 2.  Varies under both:
               reach_M_tlbA.raw and reach_M_tlbAB.raw both show C = CI.

NO NEW ROW HAS A CHANNEL THAT CANNOT VARY UNDER ITS NAMED MUTANT, and unusually the proof
is not a prediction: the channel was measured on 2026-09-09 by a separate instrument
(tlb_reach_control.py) before either row existed.

ANCHOR RULE (clause (f)) NOTE.  These are not distinctness rows -- each asserts an ABSOLUTE
value (0x11) produced by a channel outside the decode under test (the colour arm, not the
address), so they do not have the relative-suite blindness.  They are also not accept rows
at a window base or top, so the far-bound blindness does not apply either.  What they do
NOT pin is WHERE the destination lands: all three read the same address by construction, so
a translation of the tile lookup is invisible to them, exactly as it is to `fill top'.
That is unchanged by this repair and is not claimed to be closed.


5.  PRE-REGISTERED PREDICTIONS  (written before the table; a prediction copied into a
    measured column afterwards is the frozen-number class in a new coat)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
BASE is already MEASURED at 16/16 (BASE.raw, sentinel MTEFILL_DONE, PROBE_RC=0) and is the
only number in this section that is not a prediction.  Every other line is a claim this
table may refute.  The 14-row column is the MEASURED score from killtable2_20260907.

    mutant          edit at the #509 site                        14-row  predict  reasoning
    M_tlbA          && dst_tlb == MTE_TLB_A                      14/14    14/16   both new rows red
    M_tlbAB         && dst_tlb != MTE_TLB_C                      14/14    15/16   only `fill c top' red
    M1_reinstate    depth == 32 -> depth == 4                    10/14    10/16   +2 red (CI at every label)
    M2_nodst        delete dst_mode |= RGBA                       9/14     9/16   +2 red (dst never RGBA)
    M3_nosrc        delete src_mode |= RGBA                      11/14    13/16   +0: a FILL never reads src_mode
    M4_copyonly     && (mode & MTE_MODE_COPY)                    10/14    10/16   +2 red (the new rows are fills)
    M5_dstrgb       dst_mode |= RGB                              12/14    14/16   +0: RGB writes bg>>24 at dest[0]
    M6_ge16         depth >= 16                                  12/14    14/16   +0: true at 32, so RGBA
    M7_ne8          depth != 8                                   12/14    14/16   +0: same, equivalent to M6
    M8_srcrgb       src_mode |= RGB                              13/14    15/16   +0: a FILL never reads src_mode
    M9_ge32         depth >= 32                                  13/14    15/16   +0: true at 32, so RGBA
    NULL_dead       (no build -- probe run against /bin/true)      n/a      0/16   identity rows must all red

THE PREDICTION THAT MATTERS FOR THE FLOOR is the first two: both must land BELOW 16/16, and
each must be killed by exactly the rows the CHANNEL section names.  A score below 16 killed
by some OTHER row would be a wrong-reason kill and would not discharge the obligation.

THE PREDICTION THAT MATTERS FOR "THE REPAIR LOSES NO EXISTING KILL" is M1..M9: each must
red the SAME existing rows it reddened at 14, and no existing row may go green.

NULL MEDIUM: *** NONE WAS DEFINED IN killtable_20260907 OR killtable2_20260907. ***  That is
a real gap in the original tables and is recorded rather than glossed.  One is added here
(NULL_dead) because clause (c) asks for it: it costs no build, and it is the cheapest
demonstration that the three identity rows (`live', `devsig', `paint ok') are load-bearing.
It is NOT a substitute for a comment-only-file null medium, which is not run here.

